Roman Forts
-----
Roman fort design prioritized efficiency, defensibility,
and rapid deployment of standardized plans.

Descriptions 
----------------------------
Compact Desert Castellum (top: intact reconstruction; bottom: ruins):
- Square/quadriburgium plan with four projecting corner towers
- Central open courtyard
- Barracks/rooms built against inner faces of walls
- Arid desert setting with sandy terrain
- Consistent with late Roman frontier fort Qasr Bshir
  (ancient name: Castra Praetorium Mobeni / Praetorium Mobeni)
  in Jordan, built AD 293-305 under Tetrarchy (Diocletian era)
- One of the best-preserved examples of a late Roman castellum
- Roughly 56-57 m per side; towers up to ~10 m high in ruins

Larger Developed Fortress (temperate landscape):
- Extensive rectangular enclosure with multiple towers and gates
- Dense internal buildings: long barrack blocks, red-tiled roofs,
  central ranges (principia-style structures)
- Surrounding fields, roads, and possible civilian settlement (vicus)
- Green rolling hills and agricultural landscape
- Typical of auxiliary or legionary forts in northern/western provinces
  (Britain, Germania, etc.)

Name of the First Fort
----------------------
Qasr Bshir (also Qasr Bashir, Qasr Beshir)
Ancient Latin name: Castra Praetorium Mobeni (or Praetorium Mobeni)
Location: Jordan desert, Limes Arabicus frontier
Type: Late Roman quadriburgium (small square fort with corner towers)
Date: AD 293-305

Relevant Fundamentals 
-------------------------------------
Types:
- Legionary fortress (castra): large, for ~5,000 men
- Auxiliary fort (castellum): smaller, for cohorts/alae
- Fortlet / milecastle: very small outposts

Layout Variables:
- Overall shape = rectangular with rounded corners ("playing card")
- Defenses = ditches + earth rampart + wall (timber early, stone later)
- Gates = usually four (porta praetoria, principalis, etc.)
- Towers = corner + interval; projecting in later periods
- Internal = principia (HQ), praetorium (commandant's house),
  barracks, horrea (granaries), fabricae (workshops),
  valetudinarium (hospital), baths
- Surrounding = often a vicus (civilian settlement) outside walls

Deployment:
- Highly standardized across the empire
- From Scotland (Hadrian's Wall) to Syria/Jordan desert

Vindolanda Wooden Tablets 
---------------------------------------
Site: Roman auxiliary fort of Vindolanda, near Hadrian's Wall,
      Northumberland, Britain

Discovery: From 1973 onward (Robin Birley and team);
           thousands of tablets recovered from waterlogged deposits

Types:
- Ink tablets: thin postcard-sized slices of local wood
  (birch, alder, oak) written in carbon-based ink with a pen
- Stylus tablets: thicker wood with wax-filled recess,
  written with a metal stylus

Date: Mostly AD 90-120 (Trajanic period), some earlier/later

Content:
- Official military documents (strength reports, accounts)
- Personal letters between soldiers, officers, and families
- Supply requests (socks, sandals, underpants, etc.)
- Famous example: Birthday invitation (~AD 100) from Claudia Severa
  to Sulpicia Lepidina (wives of fort commanders)
  - Oldest known Latin handwriting by a woman in Britain
- Everyday details of frontier life: food, clothing, social ties,
  unit strength, and administration

Significance:
- Largest collection of Roman handwritten documents from the
  western empire
- Provides direct, unfiltered insight into daily life inside
  a Roman fort

Other Notable Roman Fort Reconstructions
----------------------------------------
Physical reconstructions:
- Saalburg (Germany): Partial full-scale reconstruction of
  a Limes Germanicus fort, including gates, barracks, and principia
- Lunt Roman Fort (near Coventry, Britain): Timber gateway and
  structures reconstructed
- Arbeia / South Shields (Britain): Reconstructed barrack block
  and centurion's house
- Vindolanda itself: Sections of Hadrian's Wall and fort features
  reconstructed nearby

Artistic / digital reconstructions:
- Numerous drawings and 3D models of Saalburg, Housesteads
  (Hadrian's Wall), Caerleon (legionary fortress), and eastern
  frontier forts including Qasr Bshir
- Common features shown: tiled roofs, timber or stone barracks,
  gate towers, and surrounding agricultural land or desert

